## Deployment Architecture

In this assignment, both the Flask backend and Express frontend are deployed on a single Amazon EC2 instance.

## EC2 Instance Setup

A free-tier EC2 instance was launched and configured with:
Python → to run Flask backend
Node.js → to run Express frontend
Git → to clone application repositories
Both applications were deployed inside the same server environment but exposed on different ports:

Application	Port
Flask Backend	5000
Express Frontend	3000
Process managers like PM2 were used to ensure applications remain running even after system restart.

## Application Flow

User → EC2 Public IP →
➡ Express Frontend (Port 3000)
➡ Flask Backend (Port 5000)

Both services run independently but on the same machine.

### CI/CD Deployment of Flask & Express on EC2:
This project demonstrates deployment of a Flask backend and Express frontend on a single Amazon EC2 instance
with an automated CI/CD pipeline using Jenkins.
Since AWS EC2 access was not available, the deployment was simulated locally using PM2 to mimic real production deployment.
The architecture and deployment flow remain the same as an actual cloud-based implementation.

## Technologies Used

Amazon EC2
Jenkins
GitHub
Python (Flask)
Node.js (Express)
PM2 (Process Manager)

## Deployment Steps
Launched EC2 Instance
Installed Python, Node.js and Git
Cloned Flask & Express repositories

## Installed dependencies:
pip install -r requirements.txt
npm install

## Configured apps on ports:

Flask → 5000
Express → 3000
Started apps using PM2

## CI/CD Pipeline

Jenkins automates deployment:
Pulls latest code from GitHub
Installs dependencies
Restarts application using PM2

## ARCHITECTURE DIAGRAM
        Developer
            ↓
         GitHub
            ↓
         Jenkins
            ↓
     Install Dependencies
            ↓
      Restart Apps (PM2)
            ↓
 ┌──────────────────────┐
 |   Local Server       |
 |                      |
 |  Flask → Port 5000   |
